  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1244 AM CDT Mon Sep 14 2026\n\nLate night water vapor imagery and RAP objective analysis showed \nquite a robust shortwave trough centered over Idaho into western \nMontana with broad southwest flow in the mid-upper troposphere \nacross much of the Rockies into adjacent Northern and Central High \nPlains. This pattern has shunted the effective North American \nMonsoon circulation to the east across much of New Mexico into \nColorado, with the northern portion of the subtropical moist \ncirculation pattern becoming infused with the polar system moving \nIdaho and Montana. \n\nThe polar system up north will move across Montana into the western \nDakotas by Monday Night, and an associated polar cold front will \npush south across Nebraska into northwestern Kansas by Monday Night. \nA sub-1000mb surface low is likely to develop out of a broad leeside \ntrough, with increasing boundary layer convergence along the lee \ntrough and especially in vicinity of surface low. Thus, the best \nfocus for late afternoon and evening thunderstorms will be out west \nacross far western Kansas. The majority of CAMs are lined up fairly \nwell with convective signal all along the surface trough, and using \nHREF ensemble probability of Echo Tops > 45 kft would support the \nnotion for at least marginally severe storms with isolated to widely \nscattered 60+ mph wind gusts possible. The strength of the \nconvection will be tied fairly closely to the diurnal cycle with \nsevere wind risk diminishing after sunset. \n\nMonday will also likely be the last upper 90s/near 100 degree day \nfor most of southwest Kansas ahead of the front, as Tuesday forecast \ntemperatures will be quite a bit lower, especially north of the \nArkansas River (i.e. Dighton highs of 97F Monday, 74F Tuesday). The \nfront will likely bisect Kansas from southwest to northeast, and by \nmid to late afternoon, there should be another round of loosely \norganized thunderstorm activity, mainly focused on our eastern and \nsoutheastern counties.",
